(a) Any person having a grievance against a person subject to this article for an alleged ethics violation shall file a sworn complaint, in writing, with the city clerk, setting forth in reasonable detail the particular facts and circumstances which constitute the alleged violation. The complainant shall support the complaint with affidavits and/or documentary evidence, if available. Respondent shall be immediately informed of the alleged violation, by the city clerk who shall transmit to respondent a copy of the complaint; respondent and shall have 30 days to file, in writing, a sworn response to the alleged violation, submitting any affidavits and/or documentary evidence in support.
(b) To determine the validity of a complaint filed against an elected official, the members of the mayor and council other than the official(s) named in the complaint shall appoint an ethics board within 30 days of the filing of the complaint. Such board shall elect one of its members as the chairman and set an initial hearing to be held not later than 15 days after the response is filed. The purpose of this hearing is solely to determine whether the complaint is meritorious, and not vexatious or imposed solely for the purpose of harassment. The board shall determine the merit of a complaint by examining the following criteria:
(1) The timing of the ethics complaint with respect to when the facts supporting the alleged violation became known or should have become known to the complainant, when the ethics complaint was filed, and the date of any pending election in which the respondent is a candidate or is involved with a candidacy, if any;
(2) The nature and type of publicity surrounding the filing of the ethics complaint, and the degree of participation by the complainant in publicizing the fact that an ethics complaint was filed;
(3) The existence and nature of any relationship between the respondent and the complainant before the ethics complaint was filed;
(4) If respondent is presently a candidate for election to office, the existence and nature of any relationship between the complainant and any candidate or group opposing the respondent;
(5) The information contained in the respondent's filing.
(6) Other evidence of the complainant's motives in filing the complaint.
If the ethics board determines the complaint to be meritorius, the ethics board shall hold a separate hearing within 30 days from the closure of the frivolity review. The board shall be appointed as provided herein.
(c) To determine the validity of the complaint against a city appointed officer, employee, or appointed member of a board or committee (other than an authority or commission in which the terms of members are fixed by law, or who are subject to other codes of ethics or laws imposing ethical standards), the mayor and council shall examine the validity of the complaint based upon the above criteria. If the complaint is found to be meritorious, the mayor and council shall appoint an ethics board, who shall hold a hearing within 30 days of their appointment.
(d) Once a complaint against a person covered by this article is filed with the city clerk, the complainant shall be compelled to submit to an examination, under oath, by the ethics board. The complainant may appear with or without counsel, at their own expense. No complaint shall be withdrawn by the complainant, after filing; provided however, the ethics board may dismiss a complaint at any point it determines the complaint to be frivolous, without completing an investigation of its substantive merits. A complaint brought solely to attain political advantage shall be deemed frivolous.
(e) No action may be taken on a complaint filed more than 180 days from the time that the violation is alleged to have occurred or a person could have known, in the exercise of reasonable diligence, the facts giving rise to a complaint, unless a person, by fraud or other device, prevents discovery of the violation. Where the allegations are the subject of a personnel proceeding or where the complainant is required to exhaust his or her administrative remedies prior to filing a complaint, the statute of limitations shall be tolled until the termination of said personnel proceeding or the exhaustion of administrative remedies.
(Ord. No. 07-02, 5-21-2007; Ord. No. 13-01, § 1, 5-17-2013)

